A||||7||Adrift||Water getting into engine compartment through scoops?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||06-30-2001||05:28 PM||||I've been pondering this whole adding side/trunk/hood scoop thing and there is a part I just don't really understand - If it rains/snows/hails and you have trunk/hood/side scoops, won't water get into your engine compartment and cause your car to rust from the inside out?<P>Just a thought,<BR>Ari<P>------------------<BR>My Dream Fiero, you ask?<BR>That would be a automatic '86 or newer V6 GT Fastback with T-Tops and A/C, fully running and for sale for under $1000 CAN. Know someone who's selling a car like this for this price in the Toronto area? PLEASE E-MAIL ME!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||06-30-2001||06:13 PM||||YES!||||reg||11
Z||000002||AkursedX||06-30-2001||06:18 PM||||I suppose yes, but water already gets into the engine bay via the decklid vents, so I don't know how much of an adverse effect those extra scoops would add.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steven||06-30-2001||06:32 PM||||And it's not like the bottom of the car is water tight. It is open to water splashing up, and snow being packed up in there. The important thing is to make sure that water is not getting in to the engine itself.<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 06-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Adrift||06-30-2001||06:34 PM||||How can water get into the actual engine?<BR>And this also occured to me - won't water evaporate on contact with any engine piece, considering they are running so hot?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steven||06-30-2001||06:44 PM||||Water by nature will seek out the lowest point. If the duct work going from the scoop (where ever it is located) to the throttle body is routed so that the source of the water is higher than the engine. And no where is there a place for the water to escape. The only place for it to go is in the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||07-01-2001||02:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>won't water get into your engine compartment <P>Just a thought,<BR>Ari<P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Who cares...<BR>Every puddle you drive over sprays water into the engine compartment...<BR>Sure, they add deflectors, but it still gets in there...<BR>If you don't want water in your engine, drain the coolant... ;-}<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-01-2001||12:30 PM||||The main thing is that water/whatever doesn't sit inside places. Sitting wet trash will get a reaction going and keep it stoked.<P>If you do a side scoop you want to make sure whatever is behind it won't hold the incomming water and that any metal is not left bare.<P>Hood or deck lid scoops aren't much of an issue the area below them is already a wet area. You just want to make sure you can't get a bunch of leaves and stuff in there that can couse a fire.||||reg||1
